    choose: [['Do not equate an outcome (with / from) personal failure.', 'with'], ['Relatives may hold a preconceived (view / viewed).', 'view'], ['The partners grew (apart / aside) over time.', 'apart'], ['She accepted that the relationship had (ended / ending).', 'ended'], ['Her sadness does not necessarily (imply / implies) a wish to reverse the decision.', 'imply'], ['The account should be attributed (to / for) Yuko.', 'to']],
    choices: [['Which distinction matters in Yuko’s account?', 'Regret about the relationship’s outcome versus acceptance of its ending.', 'Proof that one former partner must be a bad person.', 'A'], ['What is not reported?', 'Relatives’ judgement.', 'A decision to reverse the divorce.', 'B'], ['What does the interview support?', 'Her account of changing priorities and outside judgement.', 'A universal explanation of why every marriage ends.', 'A'], ['What does Daisuke’s comment add?', 'A verified rule about all generations.', 'His perspective on stigma and changing attitudes.', 'B']],
    mission: { situation: 'You are Emi. Your tutor is Leila. Listen to a new editing note about regret and outside judgement. Explain how you would preserve the meaning.', task: 'Qualify the summary, distinguish the two forms of regret and avoid inventing a private cause or future decision.', roles: ['Leila', 'Emi'], title: 'Yuko interview: meaning check', text: 'Emi, the summary should keep two ideas together: Yuko is sorry the marriage did not work, and she accepts that ending it was appropriate for her. She described different priorities, not a complete account of every private event. Do not add infidelity, abuse or a plan to reconcile when none is provided. Daisuke’s observation about the stigma of divorce belongs to his experience of social attitudes. It does not replace Yuko’s own explanation, establish a universal rule or give us permission to broadcast material merely because we discussed it.', lines: ['Emi, how could you state both parts of Yuko’s position?', 'Does regretting an unsuccessful marriage prove she regrets the divorce?', 'What causes should we avoid adding to the account?', 'How would you attribute Daisuke’s comment?', 'Does having interview material settle permission for a public episode?'], evidence: 'Distinct private message. Yuko forty-two, divorce three years earlier, relatives knew little, partners’ priorities diverged. Do not invent blame, abuse, infidelity, legal findings, reconciliation, a current partner or universal social facts. Daisuke later listens to part of interview, but publication permission is not established. Attribute his generational perspective without treating it as law or a universal historical rule.', useful: ['She regrets ..., while ...', 'That does not necessarily imply ...', 'According to her account, ...', 'Daisuke’s perspective concerns ...', 'The material does not establish ...'] },
    topics: [['NUANCED ACCOUNTS', ['How can two apparently conflicting feelings coexist?', 'Why identify exactly what a person regrets?', 'What might a short label such as failure leave out?']], ['OUTSIDE JUDGEMENT', ['Why can limited knowledge lead to overconfident conclusions?', 'How might a reporter challenge an assumption respectfully?', 'What is the difference between understanding an account and deciding who is to blame?']], ['ATTRIBUTION', ['How should a generational observation be introduced?', 'Why avoid adding a private cause that was not described?', 'What can be left open in an accurate summary?']]],
