    mission: { situation: 'You are Emi. Your tutor is Leila. Read the new planning brief. Explain how the team can explore family pressure while respecting the decision about Akari’s material.', task: 'Separate relevance from permission, identify recognition risk and describe the agreed next approach accurately.', roles: ['Leila', 'Emi'], title: 'Family-pressure episode: planning brief', text: '<p><strong>Planning brief: develop the issue without the private account</strong></p><p>Keep Akari’s disclosures out of the episode plan. Their relevance does not turn them into authorised material, and a changed name would not necessarily prevent recognition.</p><p>Develop the broader issue through general discussion and people who explicitly agree to participate. Establish the proposed use with those people before treating their contributions as available. This brief supplies no completed replacement interview or publication date.</p><p>Represent different circumstances rather than suggest that every family fits one story. The team can acknowledge an editorial mistake at the proposal stage and change direction before private words are used. The public-interest question remains worth exploring without overriding the source’s choice.</p>', lines: ['Emi, why can’t relevance alone make Akari’s disclosure available?', 'Would changing the name answer every identification concern?', 'What approach did we agree to develop?', 'What would we need to clarify with any new participant?', 'How should we avoid making one family stand for everyone?', 'Can we say a replacement episode has already been recorded or released?'], evidence: 'Fresh planning brief. Akari has no public permission; contextual identification risk survives name removal. Team excludes private material and plans consenting participants/general discussion, not a new episode already produced. Leila apologises at proposal stage; no earlier leak or breach is invented. Do not create a disguised version or fictionalised retelling of Akari’s circumstances as a workaround.', useful: ['Relevance does not establish ...', 'Even without a name, ...', 'The agreed approach is to ...', 'We would need explicit agreement about ...', 'The plan does not yet confirm ...'] },
